## Storybook — Interactive Controls

Every prop gets an `argType` control. The purpose is to:
- Toggle between all variants interactively
- Test edge cases (long text, overflow, empty states)
- Break components with extreme prop values
- Control disabled/loading/error states

```typescript
const meta: Meta<typeof Button> = {
  title: 'Atoms/Button',
  component: Button,
  tags: ['autodocs'],
  argTypes: {
    variant: { control: 'select', options: ['primary', 'secondary', 'tertiary'] },
    size: { control: 'select', options: ['sm', 'md', 'lg'] },
    disabled: { control: 'boolean' },
    children: { control: 'text' },
  },
};
```

---

## Extensibility

If a global component needs modification for a specific use case, **add a backwards-compatible prop**. Never fork the component or use one-off style overrides.

---

## What We Do NOT Use

- Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI (directly)
- Styled-components, Emotion, CSS-in-JS
- TypeScript `enum` keyword (use `as const` objects)
- TypeScript `interface` keyword (use `type` for everything)
- Default exports (ever)
- Inline styles
- Hardcoded user-visible strings
- Raw HTML text elements without `<Typography>`
- Raw pixel values in CSS (use variables)
